From 7e3354f2afde1384cfa6076e793dc4446bdd3663 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (via Anthropic sandbox)" <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 03:47:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: restore accidentally-deleted app/matching/allocator.py

CRITICAL REGRESSION FOUND: commit 5c311a9 ('Final QA sweep, hygiene
fixes') deleted app/matching/allocator.py -- the graph-based
OneToNAllocator with 3-way (Bank+Gateway+Invoice) grouping and
transitive-trap detection -- apparently because it shared a class
name with the older, much simpler subset-sum-only OneToNAllocator in
app/allocation/one_to_n.py, and was misidentified as a duplicate.

This broke the pipeline completely: app/pipeline.py:147 referenced
OneToNAllocator with no import for it (NameError on every
instantiation), and tests/test_allocator_integrity.py's import was
redirected to the wrong (incompatible) class, producing 17 more
AttributeError failures. 25 of 111 tests were failing on a fresh
clone of origin/main before this fix -- not the '107/107 passing'
or '343/450 DEV' claimed in the most recent status report, which
could not have been produced by actually running this code.

Fix:
- Restored app/matching/allocator.py (with the 0.80-threshold fix
  from the previous commit already applied).
- Added the missing import to app/pipeline.py.
- Fixed tests/test_allocator_integrity.py's import to point at the
  correct module again.

111/111 tests pass on a fresh clone after this fix.

app/allocation/one_to_n.py (the old subset-sum allocator) now has
zero remaining references anywhere in the codebase -- it is
genuinely orphaned. Left in place rather than deleted again, since
deleting same-named-but-different files without verifying zero
references is exactly what caused this regression. Recommend
renaming it before any future cleanup pass touches it.
